   "open_questions": "1) I could not see the prelim approval order itself. ECF 379 is not on RECAP: CourtListener shows only the entry 'Order AND Set Deadlines/Hearings AND Terminate Motions', created 2026-09-08. Bloomberg Law and MLex confirm the 9/8 grant. So the final approval hearing date and the claim, opt-out and objection deadlines are unknown. A secondary source says the proposed schedule puts the hearing at least 150 days after prelim approval, which would be about February 2027 or later; confirm against ECF 379 or the settlement site once it launches. 2) The task hint said ECPA, § 632.7 and FSCA were pled; the operative Doe complaint pleads none of them. ECPA (Count I) and FSCA (Count XIII) were pled only in the separate Hodges v. GoodRx case (S.D. Fla. 1:23-cv-24127-BB). That case's $13M deal was preliminarily approved, then stayed in Nov. 2023, and the case was administratively closed. The Doe release covers all claims arising from the 'Data Disclosure' or that could have been asserted on the complaint's facts. So Hodges-style ECPA and FSCA claims against GoodRx and Criteo appear to be released for class members, but they were not pled in Doe. 3) The class size is from a sealed declaration as reported in the motion (about 56.3M), not the 54.8M in the hint. 4) Prior history: a $25M GoodRx-only deal was denied without prejudice on 2025-06-12 (ECF 231); the $32M GoodRx+Criteo motion (ECF 261) was denied on 2026-01-16 (ECF 267); an Addendum narrowing the release was executed on 2026-03-24.",
