   "xcheck_notes": "Claims check (confirmed): I could not refute the researcher; I agree with every statute value. I did not get the operative Second Amended Complaint (SAC) itself, or the original complaint. On the C.D. Cal. RECAP docket (2:23-cv-03007), the state-court complaint was filed as Ex. A to the notice of removal, but it is not available there. I confirmed the claims from three primary court documents instead.\n\n(1) The federal remand order (Dkt. 23, 2023-06-27, Judge Fitzgerald), which I found on my own. It says the original complaint brings three state-law claims: CIPA Cal. Pen. Code § 631, CMIA Civ. Code § 56.10, and invasion of privacy under the California Constitution. It also calls the action non-diverse with \"purely state-law claims\". Removal was under the federal-officer statute, 28 U.S.C. § 1442(a), not federal-question jurisdiction. So no ECPA/Wiretap Act claim was pled.\n\n(2) Plaintiff's fee motion (filed 2026-07-31), § II and fn. 1. I re-downloaded it from cw.simpluris.com/docs/public/downloads/MVC2/. It says the remaining claim is CIPA \"Penal Code section 631\". It says the SAC (filed 2024-07-23; the settlement agreement says July 19, 2024) also asserted CMIA and California Constitution privacy claims. The demurrer was sustained in part on 2024-11-20, leaving only the CIPA claim. The fee motion also lists a First Amended Complaint (filed 2023-12-29) but does not list its claims.\n\n(3) The court-approved Long Form Notice, Q3, which names Cal. Pen. Code § 631(a).\n\nNone of these says § 632, § 632.7, § 638.51, 18 U.S.C. § 2511 or Fla. Stat. § 934.10. The settlement agreement, preliminary approval order and final approval order do not name any statute; the agreement only refers to the operative SAC. The Fraietta declaration billing diaries label the matter \"MemorialCare CMIA\" and mention no other statute.\n\nRemaining caveat: the SAC text was not reviewed. The fee motion (fn. 1) lists the SAC's claims and gives a single CIPA claim, and the notice names only § 631(a). That makes it very unlikely the SAC added a § 632 or § 638.51 count, but this is not verified from the pleading itself.\n\nFor the 638.51 comparison: this is a clean Meta Pixel / § 631(a) health-website settlement in LA Superior Court (complex). It survived a demurrer on the § 631 claim alone; the CMIA and Constitution claims were dismissed. | fees_requested: Requested $250,000 for fees and costs combined (one-third of the fund), which was less than the reported lodestar of $300,200 (448.8 hours). The final approval order of 2026-10-08 awarded $250,000 in full.→$250,000 for fees and costs combined (one-third of the $750,000 fund), plus a $2,500 service award. The lodestar was $300,200 for 448.8 hours as of 2026-07-31. The fee motion was filed 2026-07-31; the PA order had set a 2026-07-01 deadline, which was later extended. On 2026-10-08 the court awarded the full $250,000 and the $2,500 service award. | Timing/money check (confirmed): I could not refute this. Primary sources confirm two in-window events: preliminary approval (2026-05-22) and final approval (2026-10-08).\n\n(1) Prelim approval: the PA order is signed by Judge Laura A. Seigle and dated 05/22/2026. The final approval order and the fee motion also cite the 2026-05-22 grant.\n\n(2) Final approval: the LASC Dept. 17 minute order of 2026-10-08 grants the final approval motion, which was filed 2026-09-10. The signed Order Granting Final Approval and Final Judgment is dated 10/08/2026. Zero objections, 3 opt-outs.\n\n(3) Final hearing: the PA order first set it for 2026-09-03, then changed it by hand to 2026-09-17 at 9 a.m. It was actually heard on 2026-10-08, after the objection deadline moved from 7/15 to 8/14 and the opt-out deadline from 8/21 to 9/21. The settlement website mhspixelsettlement.com is out of date: it still shows a 9/17 hearing and says the settlement is pending final approval.\n\n(4) Prelim motion: the original motion was filed 2026-03-10, the same day the agreement was executed, per Bursor & Fisher time entries in the Fraietta declaration. That date is outside the window. The court issued a 4/13 order raising issues. An Amended Motion for Preliminary Approval was filed 2026-05-04 (in window), heard 5/7, and granted 5/22.\n\n(5) Fund: the Settlement Agreement sets a non-reversionary fund of $750,000. Class size: about 182,303 members per the Settlement Agreement. The class is everyone who logged into the MyChart portal from 3/7/2022 through 7/8/2022.\n\n(6) Claims, relevant to the user's filter: the case was filed 2023-03-07 and alleged CIPA, CMIA and California constitutional privacy claims. On demurrer (2024-11-20), the CMIA and constitutional claims were dismissed. Only the CIPA claim under Penal Code § 631 survived to settlement. There is no § 632, ECPA or Florida claim. Counsel: Bursor & Fisher and Drury Legal.\n\n(7) Post-judgment: non-appearance review is set for 2027-06-22; the final report is due 2027-06-15. The minute order says \"6/15/2026\", which is a typo; the signed order says June 15, 2027.",
   "open_questions": "(1) I did not review the SAC text directly. The claims list (§ 631, CMIA, Cal. Const. privacy) comes from the fee motion and the notice, so § 632 and § 638.51 are \"not_pled\" on that basis. (2) The 2026-03-10 filing date for the original preliminary approval motion is inferred: the billing records show the papers finalized that day and the fee motion says the parties \"promptly moved.\" On 2026-04-13 the court issued an order raising issues, and an amended motion was filed on 2026-05-04 and granted on 2026-05-22. (3) The final approval hearing moved twice: set for 2026-09-03 in the preliminary approval order, listed as 2026-09-17 on the website, held 2026-10-08. The objection deadline was extended to 2026-08-14 and the opt-out deadline to 2026-09-21. The final approval motion was filed 2026-09-10. (4) The number of claims and the per-claimant payment have not been reported, so the per-claimant amount is still unknown. (5) The SAC filing date differs by source: 2024-07-19 in the Settlement Agreement and billing records, 2024-07-23 in the fee motion.",
