   "xcheck_notes": "Claims check (confirmed): I could not refute this. I agree with the researcher on every statute.\n\n1. Settlement Agreement, read by rendering the scanned pages to images. Recital F (p. 2) says Kingsbury and Yates filed the Florida Action (26-CA-006880, Hillsborough) on 2026-06-23, alleging FSCA (Fla. Stat. § 934.03), ECPA and WESCA. Recital A (pp. 1-2) says the E.D. Pa. Yates complaint pled ECPA (18 U.S.C. § 2511(1)) and WESCA (18 Pa. C.S. § 5701). CIPA appears only in the release definition (§ 1.24, p. 8, alongside FSCA, WESCA and ECPA). It is never listed as an asserted claim.\n\n2. The long-form notice (classaction.org/media/evernorth-long-notice.pdf; text extracted) says in Q3 that the suit claims violations of FSCA § 934.03 and ECPA § 2511(1). It does not mention CIPA.\n\n3. The E.D. Pa. complaint (RECAP gov.uscourts.paed.638392.1.0) has Count I ECPA § 2511(1) and Count II WESCA § 5701. It does not cite CIPA or any California statute.\n\n4. The CourtListener docket for 2:25-cv-02773 shows no amended complaint. The sequence is: complaint, motion to dismiss denied 2025-12-10 (ECF 20), answer filed 2025-12-24 (ECF 21), then the 2026-05-27 entries. No evidence that any count was dropped.\n\n5. The amended preliminary approval order (Hillsborough) names no statutes.\n\nCaveats:\n- I did not get the Florida complaint itself. ehisettlement.com/documents does not post it. Its Motion for Preliminary Approval link returned an HTML page instead of the PDF, even through Playwright. Guessed classaction.org media URLs all returned 404. The session's web-search budget was used up, so I could not search further.\n- The FSCA claim is cited as § 934.03 (the substantive ban), not § 934.10 (the civil-remedy provision). It is still an FSCA claim.\n- Confirmation rests on the signed Settlement Agreement filed with the court (Recital F), which is a primary document. Local copies are in the scratchpad evn\\ folder. | prelim_approval_date: 2026-08-28→2026-08-18 | fees_requested: Class Counsel will request no more than one-third of the Settlement Amount (SA § 8.1) plus up to $15,000 in costs and expenses, and Defendants agreed not to oppose (clear sailing). The agreement's numeral reads \"$1,116,666.67\", but one-third of $3.5M is $1,166,666.67, which is what the long notice and secondary sources use. The fee motion is due by 2026-11-01 per the notice. Nothing has been awarded yet.→Up to one-third of the $3.5M Settlement Amount/Benefit Cap (about $1,166,666.67) plus up to $15,000 in costs, with clear sailing (SA § 8.1). The numeral in SA § 8.1 reads \"$1,116,666.67\", which is a typo. Service awards are up to $5,000 for each of the 2 plaintiffs. The fee motion is due 2026-11-01. Nothing has been awarded yet. | Timing/money check (confirmed): The in-window events are confirmed by primary documents taken from the official Kroll settlement website (ehisettlement.com/documents, reached through Playwright because of Cloudflare) and from classaction.org copies.\n\n(1) PRELIM MOTION: Plaintiffs' Unopposed Motion for Preliminary Approval carries the e-filing stamp \"Filing # 254920600 E-Filed 08/14/2026 05:31 PM, Hillsborough County/13th Judicial Circuit\". The website lists it as docket [14]. This matches the researcher's 2026-08-14.\n\n(2) CORRECTION ON THE PRELIM APPROVAL DATE: The researcher gave 2026-08-28, but that is the AMENDED order. The court first signed the ORIGINAL \"Order Granting Motion for Preliminary Approval\" (Hon. Christine Marlewski), which is \"Electronically Conformed 8/18/2026\". The original left the final hearing date blank. The AMENDED preliminary approval order is e-conformed 8/28/2026 and adds the 9:30 a.m. hearing on January 6, 2027 by Zoom. The court-approved deadlines run from 8/18: the Notice Date is 45 days later (Oct 2), the fee motion 30 days after that (Nov 1), objections and opt-outs 45 days after (Nov 16), and claims 60 days after (Dec 1). This shows 2026-08-18 is the operative preliminary approval date. Both orders fall inside the window.\n\n(3) FINAL HEARING: 2027-01-06 is confirmed by the amended order and the website. There has been no final approval.\n\n(4) MONEY: The $3.5M is a claims-made \"Settlement Benefit Cap\", not a true common fund. Defendants pay valid claims of up to $15 per claimant, plus administration costs, fees and service awards, up to $3,500,000 in total (Motion; SA ¶ 1.29; Long Notice).\n\n(5) CLASS SIZE: No number is stated. The orders and the motion say only \"hundreds of thousands\" of MDLIVE portal users (May 30, 2023 to Aug 11, 2025). The notice also requires that class members had an active Facebook or Google account.\n\n(6) CLAIMS: FSCA (Fla. Stat. § 934.03), ECPA (18 U.S.C. § 2511(1)), and Pennsylvania WESCA (18 Pa.C.S. § 5701 et seq.). FSCA and ECPA qualify. There is no CIPA claim.\n\n(7) The federal action is Yates v. Evernorth, E.D. Pa. 2:25-cv-02773 (Judge Paul S. Diamond). On 12/10/2025 the court denied the motion to dismiss (ECF 20). A Stipulation of Dismissal followed on 2026-05-27 (ECF 35, per the CourtListener API). The Kingsbury Florida complaint was filed around 2026-06-23, according to classaction.org.\n\nThe Hillsborough HOVER docket could not be reached because the connection was reset from this network, but the e-filing stamps on the filed documents serve as the primary evidence. Motion file: https://www.ehisettlement.com/files/[14] Mot. for Prelim. App. of Class Action Settlement 2026-08-14.pdf",
