   "other_claims": "Stored Communications Act, 18 U.S.C. 2702 (Count 2); intrusion upon seclusion; invasion of privacy under Cal. Const. art. I sec. 1; breach of contract; UCL (Bus. & Prof. Code 17200); CLRA (purchaser subclass); common-law fraud/deceit/misrepresentation (purchaser class); Declaratory Judgment Act, 28 U.S.C. 2201. In the litigation, only the Purchaser Class was certified, and only for breach of contract and the UCL unlawful prong (ECF 414). The court denied certification of the privacy classes (Wiretap/CIPA) and the SCA class.",
   "statute_evidence": "Fourth Amended Consolidated Class Action Complaint (ECF 141, filed 2021-08-02), the operative complaint per the prelim approval motion. It has 10 claims for relief: Count 1 Wiretap Act, 18 U.S.C. 2510 et seq.; Count 2 SCA, 18 U.S.C. 2702; Count 3 CIPA, Cal. Penal Code 632; Counts 4-10 common-law/state/DJA claims. A text search of the full complaint found no 631, 632.7, 638.51 or Fla. Stat. 934. Source: https://www.googleassistantprivacylitigation.com/assets/documents/141-4th%20Amended%20Consol%20Complaint.pdf",
   "class_definition": "Two classes. (1) Purchaser Settlement Class: US Users who bought a Google-made device from 2016-05-18 through 2026-03-19. (2) Privacy Settlement Class: US Google Assistant Users and their household members whose communications were recorded through a False Accept, or recorded and disclosed to a third-party review vendor, during the same period. Exclusions include prior opt-outs and individuals pursuing arbitration who sign individual releases.",
   "class_size": 266000000,
   "class_size_note": "Estimated and disputed. About 266 million is A.B. Data's assumed notice population for the Privacy class (prelim motion ECF 549 at 7). Class counsel estimate at most 60 million Purchaser class members, and Google disputes the class size. Direct email notice went to about 173,814,291 valid addresses (final approval motion ECF 567), with about 161.9 million reminder emails. As of 2026-09-17 there were 1,536,964 claims (1,494,467 timely) and 216 valid exclusions.",
   "settlement_structure": "Non-reversionary common fund with claims-based, pro rata point distribution. If feasible, a second distribution goes to claimants; any remainder goes to cy pres recipients that require court approval.",
   "fund_amount": 68000000,
   "payment_terms": "Pro rata point system:\n- Purchaser class: 4 points per Google-made device, up to 3 devices (12 points).\n- Privacy class: 1 point.\n- Members of both classes: up to 13 points.\n- Per-point value = net fund divided by total valid points.\n- Pre-claims estimates in the prelim motion: $18-$56 per device for purchasers and $2-$10 for privacy class members.\n- Claims require a device serial number; purchaser claims also require proof of purchase.\n- Payment by digital method or check.\n- Claim deadline was 2026-08-27.",
   "fees_requested": "Fees requested: one-third of the $68M fund, or $22,666,666.67. Also $1,021,738.40 in expenses and four $10,000 service awards ($40,000 total) (ECF 566, filed 2026-07-23). Lodestar is $22,477,337.50, a 1.01 multiplier. Class members objected to the fees: Thomas (ECF 561), Kirkley (562-1), Cook (563), Smith (569), Abbatecola (570), Goldstone (571), plus one sealed objection (572/573). Nothing awarded yet. The motion was taken under submission at the 2026-10-01 hearing (ECF 577). The court ordered combined timesheets on 2026-10-07 (ECF 582), and plaintiffs filed a combined hours chart the same day (ECF 583).",
   "service_award": "$10,000 requested for each of the four plaintiffs ($40,000 total); not yet awarded.",
   "injunctive_relief": "None. Settlement Agreement para. 3.2 says Google does not agree to provide any injunctive or prospective relief.",
